---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import buildInfo from '../data/meta.json';
import programs from '../data/programs.json';
import tax from '../data/taxonomy.json';
import { DIMS, type Program, type Taxonomy } from '../lib/data';
import { SITE_NAME, OWNER, REPO_URL, SIBLING } from '../lib/site';
const T = tax as unknown as Taxonomy;
const P = (programs as Program[]).slice().sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const sections = [
  ['what', 'What this site is'],
  ['use', 'How to use it'],
  ['matching', 'How matching works'],
  ['network', 'Faculty network'],
  ['data', 'What data is included'],
  ['labels', 'Research labels'],
  ['rankings', 'Rankings'],
  ['privacy', 'Privacy'],
  ['limits', 'Limits'],
  ['contact', 'Corrections and contact'],
  ['universities', 'Universities included'],
];
---
<Base title="About" description={`What the ${SITE_NAME} is, how to use it, how matching works and where the data comes from.`}>
  <div class="about">
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="toc-h">On this page</p>
      <ol>{sections.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol>
    </nav>
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>About</h1>

      <section id="what">
        <h2>What this site is</h2>
        <p>A free tool for people applying to US PhD programs in linguistics. You describe your research interests, and it suggests faculty and programs whose work is closest to yours.</p>
        <p>It covers {buildInfo.faculty} faculty at {buildInfo.programs} universities: linguistics departments, plus related departments where linguists also supervise PhDs (computational linguistics, applied linguistics and second language acquisition, language departments, and speech, hearing and cognitive science).</p>
      </section>

      <section id="use">
        <h2>How to use it</h2>
        <dl class="uses">
          <dt><a href={base}>Find matches</a></dt><dd>Describe what you want to study in a few sentences or a paragraph. Mention your questions, methods, and the languages or populations you work with. Results can be shown by person or by program.</dd>
          <dt><a href={`${base}programs/`}>Programs</a></dt><dd>Browse universities on a map or in a list, and see each one’s departments and faculty.</dd>
          <dt><a href={`${base}faculty/`}>Faculty</a></dt><dd>Search and filter everyone by subfield, method, data, population and language.</dd>
          <dt><a href={`${base}shortlist/`}>Saved</a></dt><dd>Faculty you saved, with your notes. You can download the list as a CSV file.</dd>
        </dl>
        <p><b>Tips.</b> Specific descriptions match better than general ones: “tone sandhi in Wu Chinese using acoustic analysis” works better than “phonology”. You can write in English or Chinese. Under the search box, “We read this as” shows the subfields, methods and languages picked up from your text; click one to require it, and click again to ignore it.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="matching">
        <h2>How matching works</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Meaning.</b> Your text is turned into a numeric representation (an “embedding”) on your own device and compared with a representation of each faculty member’s research.</li>
          <li><b>Keywords.</b> Exact terms, such as language names or technical terms, are matched as well, since they often matter most in linguistics.</li>
          <li><b>Your priorities.</b> Subfields, methods and languages found in your text give a small boost to faculty who work on them, or become requirements if you click them.</li>
          <li><b>Second check.</b> The top 30 results are re-read against your text by a second model and re-ordered.</li>
          <li><b>Fit score.</b> Each person gets a score from 0 to 100. A program’s score combines its three best matches, so departments with several good fits rank above departments with one.</li>
        </ol>
        <details class="tech">
          <summary>Technical details</summary>
          <ul>
            <li>Embeddings: <code>snowflake-arctic-embed-m-v1.5</code> (768 dimensions), run in the browser with Transformers.js. Faculty vectors are precomputed and stored as 8-bit integers. The model is downloaded once (about 110 MB) and cached.</li>
            <li>Each faculty vector averages three views: the research summary with keywords, the department profile text, and the published summary, research areas and labels. Profile text is used only to compute the vector and is never published.</li>
            <li>Keyword score: BM25 over each person’s published text. Semantic and keyword scores are standardised per query and combined 85% / 15%.</li>
            <li>Re-ranking: <code>ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6</code> cross-encoder over the top 30, blended evenly with the first-pass order.</li>
            <li>Chinese input is translated to English on your device (the browser’s built-in translator when available, otherwise a 110 MB model), with a glossary of linguistic terms and language names. The translation is shown with the results so you can correct it.</li>
            <li>Scores are calibrated on 32 example descriptions across the field: the median person for a typical query scores 0 and the top 0.5% score 85. Program score = 50% best match + 30% second + 20% third.</li>
            <li>Evaluation scripts: <a href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/main/pipeline/eval`} rel="noreferrer">pipeline/eval</a>.</li>
          </ul>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="network">
        <h2>Faculty network</h2>
        <p>The <a href={`${base}network/`}>Network</a> page and the “Similar research” list on each faculty page compare every faculty member’s research profile with everyone else’s, using the same profile vectors as matching. People are placed close together when their profiles are similar, and each person is linked to their nearest neighbours. Colour shows the main subfield label.</p>
        <p>Co-author links come from <a href="https://openalex.org" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">OpenAlex</a>, an open database of scholarly publications. Each faculty member is matched to their OpenAlex author profile by name and university, and two people are linked when they appear together on a paper. Papers with more than 25 authors are left out, because sharing one of those does not mean two people worked together. Matching by name can miss people or, rarely, pick the wrong profile; corrections are welcome.</p>
        <p>Similarity is read from public profile text only. Two people being close does not mean they work together, and people with short profiles can land in odd places.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="data">
        <h2>What data is included</h2>
        <h3>Programs</h3>
        <p>Every US university with a research PhD in linguistics, starting from the <a href="https://www.linguisticsociety.org/" rel="noreferrer">Linguistic Society of America</a>’s program directory and checked against each university’s own pages. Related departments are included when they grant a research PhD and some of their faculty work on language. Every department is labelled as either a linguistics department or a related department.</p>
        <h3>Faculty</h3>
        <p>Assistant, associate and full professors, the people who can usually supervise a dissertation. Lecturers, teaching and clinical faculty, emeriti, visiting and affiliated faculty are not included. In related departments, only faculty whose research involves language are kept. Some departments do not list job titles; their faculty appear as “Rank not listed”.</p>
        <h3>What is shown about each person</h3>
        <p>A one-sentence research summary written for this site, the research areas the department lists, research labels, and links to the department profile and, when available, a personal site or Google Scholar. No photos are shown.</p>
        <p>The data was collected from official department websites on {buildInfo.built}.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="labels">
        <h2>Research labels</h2>
        <p>Each person has labels in five groups, assigned automatically by a language model from their public profile. A ★ marks their main focus. Labels are a guide, not a full account of anyone’s work, and they can be wrong.</p>
        <div class="label-groups">
          {DIMS.map((d) => <div class="panel"><b>{T.dimensions[d].label}</b><div class="chips" style="margin-top:10px">{Object.entries(T.dimensions[d].labels_short).map(([c, l]) => <span class="chip" data-dim={d} title={T.dimensions[d].labels[c]}>{l}</span>)}</div></div>)}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="rankings">
        <h2>Rankings</h2>
        <p>You can filter programs by two rankings. Both filters are off by default, and neither affects how matches are scored.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>QS Linguistics</b>: the <a href="https://www.topuniversities.com/university-subject-rankings/linguistics" rel="noreferrer">QS World University Rankings by Subject 2026: Linguistics</a> (published March 2026). It is a world ranking, so the positions are global. QS gives exact positions up to 100 and bands after that (101–150, 151–200, …). Universities outside its 350 entries are shown as “not ranked”.</li>
          <li><b>U.S. News</b>: the <a href="https://www.usnews.com/best-colleges/rankings/national-universities" rel="noreferrer">U.S. News Best National Universities 2027</a> (published September 2026). This ranks whole universities, mostly on undergraduate measures; U.S. News does not rank linguistics PhD programs. Positions below 100 are shown as “below 100”.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Rankings are a rough guide. They say little about whether a department has faculty working on what you want to study, so use them alongside the matches, not instead of them.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="privacy">
        <h2>Privacy</h2>
        <p>Matching runs entirely in your browser. Nothing you type is sent anywhere. Saved faculty and notes stay in your browser. There are no accounts and no analytics.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="limits">
        <h2>Limits</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>People with short public profiles are harder to match, so they may rank lower than they should.</li>
          <li>Departments change. Hires, departures and title changes after {buildInfo.built} are not reflected yet.</li>
          <li>Related departments (for example computer science, education, psychology, speech and hearing) admit students through their own PhD programs. Someone there may not be able to supervise a linguistics PhD, so check each department’s admissions page.</li>
          <li>Matching works in English. Chinese is translated first, so check the translation shown with your results.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="contact">
        <h2>Corrections and contact</h2>
        <p>Each faculty page has a “Report an error” link. Faculty who want their information changed or removed can <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues/new`} rel="noreferrer">open an issue</a> or email <a href={`mailto:${OWNER.email}`}>{OWNER.email}</a>.</p>
        <p>Made by <a href={OWNER.url} rel="noreferrer">{OWNER.name}</a>, based on the <a href={SIBLING.url} rel="noreferrer">{SIBLING.name}</a>. The code and data pipeline are public on <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noreferrer">GitHub</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="universities">
        <h2>Universities included</h2>
        <ul class="unis">{P.map((p) => <li><a href={`${base}programs/${p.slug}/`}>{p.name}</a> <span class="muted small">{p.n}</span></li>)}</ul>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
<style is:global>
  .about { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 220px minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 48px; align-items: start; }
  .toc { position: sticky; top: calc(var(--header-h) + 24px); }
  .toc-h { font-size: 0.8125rem; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink-3); margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .toc ol { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 2px; border-left: 2px solid var(--line); }
  .toc a { display: block; padding: 6px 12px; margin-left: -2px; border-left: 2px solid transparent; color: var(--ink-3); text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.92rem; }
  .toc a:hover { color: var(--ink); }
  .toc a[aria-current="true"] { color: var(--action-text); border-left-color: var(--action); font-weight: 600; }
  .about .prose { max-width: 72ch; }
  .about .prose section { scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--header-h) + 16px); padding-top: 8px; }
  .about .prose section + section { margin-top: 28px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding-top: 28px; }
  .about .prose h2 { margin-top: 0; }
  .uses { display: grid; grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr; gap: 10px 20px; margin: 12px 0 14px; }
  .uses dt { font-weight: 600; } .uses dd { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .tech { margin-top: 14px; padding: 14px 16px; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--bg-2); }
  .tech summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .tech ul { margin-top: 10px; }
  .label-groups { display: grid; gap: 10px; margin-top: 12px; }
  .unis { columns: 2; column-gap: 24px; list-style: none; padding: 0 !important; }
  .unis li { break-inside: avoid; padding: 3px 0; }
  @media (max-width: 860px) { .about { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px; } .toc { position: static; } .toc ol { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; border: 0; } .toc a { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 999px; padding: 6px 12px; margin: 0; } .uses { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2px 0; } .uses dd { margin-bottom: 10px; } .unis { columns: 1; } }
</style>
<script>
  const links = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('.toc a'));
  const obs = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
    for (const e of entries) if (e.isIntersecting) links.forEach((a) => a.setAttribute('aria-current', String(a.hash === '#' + e.target.id)));
  }, { rootMargin: '-20% 0px -70% 0px' });
  document.querySelectorAll('.about section[id]').forEach((s) => obs.observe(s));
</script>
